// First Approach {O(N)}
class Solution {
public:
    int largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {
        int n = heights.size();
        int max_area = 0;
        stack<int> st;
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++){
            int current_height = (i==n) ? 0 : heights[i];
            while (!st.empty() && heights[st.top()] > current_height){
                int height = heights[st.top()];
                st.pop();
                int width = st.empty() ? i : (i - st.top() - 1);
                max_area = max(max_area, height * width);
            } 
            st.push(i);
        }
        return max_area;
    }
};

- Core Idea: Instead of checking every pair of boundaries (brute force), find the maximum rectangle for every single bar acting as the shortest bar (the bottleneck).
- The Trick: A bar can only extend its rectangle left and right until it hits a bar that is strictly shorter than itself. We use a Monotonically Increasing Stack to find these boundaries efficiently.
- 1. Key Concepts
  Left Boundary (L): The first index to the left with a height strictly less than the current bar.
  Right Boundary (R): The first index to the right with a height strictly less than the current bar.
  Width Formula: When a bar is popped, its width is calculated as: {Width} = R - L - 1
  Special Case (Empty Stack): If there is no smaller element to the left, the stack becomes empty after popping. This means the bar can extend all the way to index 0, so L = -1, simplifying the formula to:{Width} = R
- 2. The Mechanics (Step-by-Step)
  Maintain an Increasing Stack: Store indices of bars in the stack. We only want heights to get taller as we go up the stack.
  Triggering a Calculation: As we loop forward, if heights[i] is shorter than the bar at the top of the stack (heights[st.top()]), a boundary is found!The current index i acts as the Right Boundary (R) for the bar at the top of the stack.
  Popping and Measuring: Pop the top bar from the stack; let its height be h.Look at the new top of the stack. That index is its Left Boundary (L).
  Calculate area: {Area} = h * (i - {st.top()} - 1) (or just h * i if the stack is now empty).
  Update max_area.
  Repeat until the current bar is no longer shorter than the top of the stack.
  Push: Push the current index i onto the stack.
- 3. Crucial Optimization Optimizations
  The Dummy Bar (i == n): The loop runs up to i <= n. When i == n, we simulate a dummy bar of height 0. 
  Because 0 is shorter than everything, it forces the stack to pop and calculate the area for all remaining bars that reached the end of the histogram.
  Strict Comparison (> vs >=): We use heights[st.top()] > current_height. This allows duplicate heights to sit together on the stack, preventing redundant/incomplete calculations and ensuring the full width is calculated when the last duplicate is popped.

// Brute Force {O(N²)}
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int largest_rect (vector<int>h){
    int max_a = 0;
    int n = h.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int min_h = INT_MAX;
        for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
            min_h = min(min_h, h[j]);
            int width = j - i + 1;
            int area = min_h * width;
            max_a = max(max_a, area);
        }
    }
    return max_a;
}
int main(){
    vector<int> h={2,1,5,6,2,3};
    cout<<largest_rect(h);
    return 0;
}
